---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { ORGANIZATION } from '../data/organization';

// AboutPage JSON-LD carries the one Organization node (src/data/organization.ts),
// the same @id the home page and iispeed.com use. Cross-property identity lets Google
// reconcile the same legal entity wherever it appears.
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About — mod_pagespeed',
  url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/about/',
  description:
    'The team behind mod_pagespeed and its 2.0 re-architecture, introduced as ModPageSpeed 2.0: We-Amp B.V., a Dutch software company founded in 2012 that helped build ngx_pagespeed and maintained mod_pagespeed, and today maintains the PageSpeed optimization lineage — led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache PageSpeed (incubating) committer who release-managed its only Apache release, 1.14.36.1, and an Apache Traffic Server PMC member since 2012.',
  mainEntity: ORGANIZATION,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — mod_pagespeed"
  description="Built by We-Amp B.V. — a Dutch web-optimization company that helped build ngx_pagespeed and maintains mod_pagespeed, home of IISpeed's IIS integration."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  {/* Hero */}
  <PageHero title="The team behind mod_pagespeed." width="prose">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      mod_pagespeed is built by We-Amp B.V., a Dutch software company founded in 2012 in Castricum,
      on the coast north of Amsterdam. It's led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache PageSpeed
      (incubating) committer and Apache Traffic Server PMC member since 2012, who helped build
      ngx_pagespeed, maintained mod_pagespeed, and release-managed 1.14.36.1, the project's only
      Apache release. One product in two parts, one shared optimization core, and a line that traces
      back to the original mod_pagespeed in 2010.
    </Fragment>
  </PageHero>
  <div class="container-prose">
    <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  {/* Lineage */}
  <section class="container-prose section">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">The lineage</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        mod_pagespeed was an open-source server-side optimization framework originally built and
        released by Google in 2010. The original team &mdash; Joshua Marantz, Jeff Kaufman, Maks
        Orlovich, Bryan McQuade and others &mdash; shipped an Apache module that did things the rest
        of the web hadn't figured out yet: server-side image transcoding, critical-CSS inlining,
        cache-extension, JS minification, dozens of transforms across a content pipeline that
        thousands of operators put in front of production traffic.
      </p>
      <p>
        It set the standard. It's no longer actively developed upstream &mdash; Google moved the
        project to the Apache Incubator in 2017, and the podling retired in 2023. The{' '}
        <a href="/psol/" class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">
          optimization libraries from the original mod_pagespeed
        </a>{' '}
        are the shared core of both parts. The{' '}
        <a href="/" class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">
          mod_pagespeed module
        </a>{' '}
        carries the proven line forward in-process on Apache, nginx and IIS; the 2.0 re-architecture
        wrapped the same rewriters in a worker and cache architecture, and that worker is the other
        part. The rewriters that decide how to compress an image or rewrite a stylesheet are the
        ones the original team built and proved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="container-prose">
    <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  {/* The company */}
  <section class="container-prose section">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">The company</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        We-Amp B.V. is a Dutch private limited company, registered at the KvK under 57898138,
        founded in Castricum in 2012. The specialty has always been web performance:{' '}
        <a
          href="/self-hosted-image-optimization/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
        >
          image optimization
        </a>
        , content rewriting, cache architecture, the boring infrastructure that decides whether a
        page is fast or slow.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today We-Amp is led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache PageSpeed (incubating) committer and
        Apache Traffic Server PMC member who helped build ngx_pagespeed and drove the project's
        Apache incubation. After the podling retired and upstream went quiet, We-Amp carried the
        lineage forward. We-Amp is{' '}
        <a
          href="https://developers.googleblog.com/en/speed-up-your-sites-with-pagespeed-for-nginx/"
          rel="nofollow noopener"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
        >
          credited by Google as an ngx_pagespeed contributor
        </a>
        . The codebase has shipped optimization code since Google's first release in 2010, and
        We-Amp has worked on it since 2012, across Apache, nginx, Envoy and IIS ports of the 1.x
        line. The 2.0 re-architecture was We-Amp's independent rebuild, and the current
        mod_pagespeed brings it together with the module as one product: one shared optimization
        core, one Cyclone Cache library underneath both parts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Self-hosted software fits the way we work: you run the binary on your infrastructure, you
        control the upgrade timing, we don't sit in your request path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside the product, We-Amp takes on consulting for teams whose question is their site
        rather than the software: a fixed-price performance and configuration audit, and longer
        engagements on caching and reverse-proxy infrastructure. See{' '}
        <a
          href="https://we-amp.com/consulting/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          data-umami-event="cta_commercial"
          data-umami-event-offer="consulting"
          data-umami-event-surface="about"
          data-umami-event-position="company_section"
        >
          consulting at we-amp.com
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="container-prose">
    <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  {/* Where AI fits */}
  <section class="container-prose section">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Where AI fits, and where it doesn't</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        Inside the binary: small LightGBM models &mdash; one per format, JPEG, WebP, AVIF &mdash;
        predict the encoder quality that hits a target SSIMULACRA2 perceptual score. Compiled to C
        through TL2cgen, around five microseconds per call. No model downloads, no runtime
        dependency, no outbound call in the request path. Every variant is verified against the
        original with SSIMULACRA2 before it reaches your cache. Don't want it?
        <code>--no-learned-quality-jpeg</code>, <code>--no-learned-quality-webp</code>,
        <code>--no-learned-quality-avif</code>. Per format.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the binary: agents watch security advisories, performance research and upstream
        changes, and brief the team. A human approves before anything user-visible ships. No release
        goes out without that sign-off.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="container-prose">
    <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  {/* Why this matters */}
  <section class="container-prose section">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Why self-hosted, in 2026</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        The 2026 web is HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, container orchestration, nginx in front of nearly
        everything, AVIF in every browser worth optimizing for. The performance story is no longer
        "the network is slow" &mdash; it's "your origin is doing too much work and shipping too many
        bytes." Server-side optimization should live next to your origin, not in someone else's data
        center two hops away.
      </p>
      <p>
        mod_pagespeed reflects that reality. The module serves and the optimizer worker optimizes;
        with the worker paired to the Apache or nginx module, and in the reverse-proxy deployment,
        they talk over a local socket, and neither phones home with request data. There is no
        third-party proxy in the path. There is no CDN dependency. It makes no outbound request of
        its own accord — the only egress is what the operator configures.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operators in 2026 have absorbed the trade-offs of edge platforms and managed services, and a
        growing number want some things back on their own boxes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="container-prose">
    <div class="rule-ticked-scale" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>

  {/* Get in touch */}
  <section class="container-prose section">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Get in touch</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-text-muted">
      Questions about the product, the company, or the lineage &mdash; email or use the form. The
      software is free; support is sold in three tiers, with prices on the pricing page.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn-primary">
        {' '}
        Contact{' '}
      </a>
      <a
        href="/pricing/"
        data-umami-event="cta_commercial"
        data-umami-event-offer="support"
        data-umami-event-surface="about"
        data-umami-event-position="get_in_touch"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-border-strong px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-text-body hover:bg-bg-secondary transition-colors"
      >
        See pricing
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10 text-xs text-text-annotation max-w-2xl">
      mod_pagespeed is an open-source project originally developed at Google. The current
      mod_pagespeed is developed by We-Amp B.V. and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Google.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
